From 2dfbcbd51360ec632ad1884aa4d83c3897b5acaa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Zaugg <jzaugg@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 01:27:22 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] Merkle: find upstream ancestors nested in objects or reached
 through one

The descendant rules for an upstream change read a descendant's
ancestors from its stored linearization, by source name, and look their
APIs up among the upstream classes, which are keyed by binary name. For
a class nested in an object the two differ, `p.O.A` against `p.O$A`, so
the lookup failed and the `abstract` rule never saw the member deferred
there. With `object O { abstract class A { def m: Int } }` and
`object Q { abstract class B extends O.A { def m = 1 } }` upstream,
removing B.m left `class C extends Q.B` downstream uncompiled, though it
no longer implements m. A parent reached through an object,
`class C extends Inst.B` with `object Inst extends Outer` and B a member
class of Outer, is stored as `p.Inst.B`, which no class is called, so
none of C's ancestors were found at all.

HierarchyView now looks an ancestor up by its binary name candidates as
well, and takes a descendant's upstream parents from the external
inheritance relation, which names their classes, along with their own
stored linearizations. Ancestors are all by source name, and `conflicts`
drops the changed class from the other paths under either name: with the
lookup fixed, an upstream nested class, named by its binary name in the
change, would otherwise count as on another path to itself, and
`conflicts` would fire for every descendant.

Composition had the same lookup: Lookup.ownAnalyzedClass, which finds
an ancestor's API in another analysis, asked for the class file of the
source name. It now tries the binary name candidates.

merkle-x-abstract-ancestor-nested and merkle-x-abstract-ancestor-instance
are merkle-x-abstract-ancestor with the ancestors nested in objects, and
members of a class inherited through an object; both fail without this
change. merkle-x-abstract-nested, merkle-x-abstract with the ancestor in
an object, passes either way.
